    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-87243",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14228212/assets/fa8eef8fc185109d90dcc6c17f9684db06050d9b2cf63560186c45e8ce0ac204.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=f(3(x+2))?$",
      "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=f(3(x+2)),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(3)}=\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(3x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14228212/assets/3027f0943e238d63996c8d1ff36d36b14bb60c18957168f7e54bb0a7ddcd9d33.png)\n\n  \n\n- Then, translate it left by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=f(3(x+2)).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14228212/assets/e3e552a5bacd539ee409a69be7e69cddc4e4e2404bcc4adc5173e980e8c8ca93.png)",
